[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"JUnit 5: Test Discovery, Execution, and Console Launcher"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"JUnit 5 provides a powerful modular architecture consisting of JUnit Platform, JUnit Jupiter, and JUnit Vintage. The JUnit Platform serves as a foundation for launching testing frameworks on the JVM. This tutorial explores how test discovery works, how tests are executed programmatically, and how to use the JUnit Console Launcher for CI/CD pipelines."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Test Discovery"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Test discovery is the process of scanning your project's classpath to identify test classes and methods annotated with JUnit annotations like @Test. The LauncherDiscoveryRequestBuilder API is used to configure discovery requests programmatically."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"LauncherDiscoveryRequest request = LauncherDiscoveryRequestBuilder.request()
    .selectors(selectPackage("com.example.project"))
    .filters(includeClassNamePatterns(".*Test"))
    .build();"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Programmatic Test Execution"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once you have created a discovery request, you can execute the tests programmatically using the Launcher API. You can also register custom TestExecutionListeners to capture events during the test lifecycle."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"Launcher launcher = LauncherFactory.create();
SummaryGeneratingListener listener = new SummaryGeneratingListener();
launcher.registerTestExecutionListeners(listener);
launcher.execute(request);
TestExecutionSummary summary = listener.getSummary();
System.out.println("Successful tests: " + summary.getTestsSucceededcount());"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Using the JUnit Console Launcher"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Console Launcher is a command-line Java application that lets you run tests from the console. It is packaged inside the junit-platform-console-standalone JAR, making it easy to integrate into build scripts or CI/CD pipelines."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Download the standalone JAR from Maven Central."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Place your compiled test classes and dependencies on the classpath."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Run the command using the Java runtime."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"java -jar junit-platform-console-standalone-1.9.3.jar --execute --class-path bin --select-package com.example"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Advanced Console Launcher Options"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Console Launcher supports multiple flags to customize test reporting and filtering. You can generate XML reports compatible with CI systems like Jenkins using the --reports-dir option."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"java -jar junit-platform-console-standalone-1.9.3.jar --execute --class-path build/classes/java/main:build/classes/java/test --scan-class-path --reports-dir build/test-results"},{"order":18,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering JUnit's discovery, execution, and console launcher gives you fine-grained control over your testing workflows. Whether you are building a custom test runner in Java or executing tests headless in a Docker container, these tools ensure reliable and scalable test automation."}]
